{
  "id": "ss.enemy.warden",
  "name": "Warden",
  "description": "A soldier bred as a door. One tapering keystone — a flat face of packed earth at the front, narrowing to nothing behind — so the silhouette is a cork, and nothing else in the game is a straight-edged wedge. The face is read in horizontal strata and is the colour of the ground it spent its life filling, which is a problem solved with a bone rim right round it rather than avoided. Under it is the acid vent, teal because what leaves it is `ss.enemy.shot`. Nothing on the body moves. The spines are nine separate parts set along the keystone's own top edge on that edge's normal, and they are static, as is the face and everything on it — an earlier cut had the row as one zig-zag polygon travelling on a track that ran opposite to the body's, so it came away from the back it sits on every cycle. Holding still is also the only arrangement in which `enraged` can split the face along its strata without the split lines sliding off, because a part a variant adds has no track in this animation to move with. The walking is done by the legs, which are chains — coxa, femur, tibia, claw — solved offline, since a part's `rot` turns it about its own origin and this format has no parenting: each segment carries an absolute rotation plus the offset that puts it back on the end of the one above it. Front legs reach forward, rear legs trail back. Drawn facing +x, the game flips it. Gameplay radius 42.",
  "tags": ["ss-enemy", "boss"],
  "size": [104, 96],
  "meta": {"radius": 42},
